# 1️⃣ Introduction

A **Virtual Environment** is an isolated Python environment for a project.

It allows each project to have:

- Its own Python packages
- Independent package versions
- Separate dependencies

This prevents conflicts between different projects.

---

# 2️⃣ Why Virtual Environments?

Imagine you have two projects.

## Project A

Uses

```
NumPy 2.0
Pandas 2.3
```

---

## Project B

Uses

```
NumPy 1.24
TensorFlow 2.12
```

If both use the same global Python installation,

upgrading NumPy for one project may break the other.

Virtual environments solve this problem.

---

# 3️⃣ What is a Virtual Environment?

Without Virtual Environment

```
Computer

│

Python

├── NumPy 2.0

├── Pandas

├── TensorFlow
```

Every project shares the same packages.

---

With Virtual Environment

```
Computer

│

├── Project A

│      Python

│      NumPy 2.0

│      Pandas

│

├── Project B

       Python

       NumPy 1.24

       TensorFlow
```

Each project has its own isolated environment.

---

# 4️⃣ Creating a Virtual Environment

Navigate to your project folder.

```bash
cd AI_Project
```

Create the virtual environment.

```bash
python -m venv venv
```

Explanation

```
python
```

Runs the Python interpreter.

```
-m
```

Runs a module.

```
venv
```

Python's built-in virtual environment module.

The last

```
venv
```

is the folder name.

You may also use

```bash
python -m venv env
```

or

```bash
python -m venv myenv
```

although `venv` is the most common naming convention.

---

# 5️⃣ Folder Structure

After creation,

```
AI_Project/

│

├── venv/

├── main.py
```

Inside the virtual environment

```
venv/

├── Include/

├── Lib/

├── Scripts/

├── pyvenv.cfg
```

Python automatically creates these files.

---

# 6️⃣ Activating the Virtual Environment

### Windows (Command Prompt)

```cmd
venv\Scripts\activate
```

---

### Windows (PowerShell)

```powershell
venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

---

### Linux / macOS

```bash
source venv/bin/activate
```

---

After activation

```
(venv)
```

appears before your terminal prompt.

Example

```
(venv) C:\Users\Username\AI_Project>
```

This indicates the virtual environment is active.

---

# 7️⃣ Installing Packages

Once activated,

install packages normally.

```bash
pip install numpy
```

```bash
pip install pandas
```

```bash
pip install matplotlib
```

```bash
pip install scikit-learn
```

Packages are installed only inside the active virtual environment.

---

# 8️⃣ Viewing Installed Packages

Display installed packages.

```bash
pip list
```

Example

```
Package        Version

numpy          2.0.1

pandas         2.3.1

matplotlib     3.x.x
```

---

# 9️⃣ requirements.txt

Professional Python projects include a

```
requirements.txt
```

file.

Create it using

```bash
pip freeze > requirements.txt
```

Example

```
numpy==2.0.1

pandas==2.3.1

matplotlib==3.10.0

scikit-learn==1.7.0
```

Another developer installs everything using

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

---

# 🔟 Sharing Projects

Never send your virtual environment folder.

Instead share

```
Project/

│

├── requirements.txt

├── README.md

├── source code
```

Other developers simply run

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

to recreate the environment.

---

# 1️⃣1️⃣ Deactivating the Virtual Environment

When finished,

```bash
deactivate
```

The terminal changes from

```
(venv)

C:\Users\Username\Project>
```

to

```
C:\Users\Username\Project>
```

The environment is now inactive.

---

# 1️⃣2️⃣ Best Practices

✔ Create one virtual environment per project.

✔ Always activate the environment before installing packages.

✔ Keep `requirements.txt` updated.

✔ Use meaningful project names.

✔ Install only necessary packages.

✔ Recreate environments instead of copying them.

---

# 1️⃣3️⃣ Common Mistakes

## ❌ Installing packages globally

Instead,

activate the virtual environment first.

---

## ❌ Uploading the `venv/` folder to GitHub

Never upload

```
venv/
```

Add it to

```
.gitignore
```

Example

```
venv/

__pycache__/

*.pyc
```

---

## ❌ Forgetting to activate the environment

If `(venv)` is missing from the terminal,

packages may install globally.

---

## ❌ Forgetting `requirements.txt`

Without it,

other developers cannot easily recreate your environment.

---

# 1️⃣4️⃣ Real-World AI Project Structure

```
AI_Project/

│

├── venv/

├── data/

├── notebooks/

├── models/

├── utils/

├── train.py

├── requirements.txt

├── README.md

└── .gitignore
```

This is the standard structure for many AI and Machine Learning projects.

